[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m $f_X(x) = \lambda e^{-\lambda x} = 4e^{-4x}$ for $x \geq 0$.
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m Now we need to find the density function of $Z = e^X$. To do this, we can use the change of variables method. Let $y = e^x$, then $x = \ln(y)$. The derivative of $\ln(y)$ with respect to $y$ is $\frac{1}{y}$. So the density function of $Z$ is given by:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m $f_Z(z) = f_X(\ln(z)) \left| \frac{d}{dz}\ln(z) \right| = 4e^{-4\ln(z)} \frac{1}{z} = 4z^{-4}\frac{1}{z} = 4z^{-5}$ for $z \geq 1$.
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m Now that we have the density function of $Z$, we can find its expected value $E(Z)$:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m $E(Z) = \int_1^\infty z f_Z(z) dz = \int_1^\infty z(4z^{-5}) dz = \int_1^\infty 4z^{-4} dz$.
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m To evaluate this integral, we can use the power rule for integration:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m $E(Z) = 4\int_1^\infty z^{-4} dz = 4\left[\frac{z^{-3}}{-3}\right]_1^\infty = 4\left[-\frac{z^{-3}}{3}\right]_1^\infty$.
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m Now we evaluate the antiderivative at the bounds:
