[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=210653, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m [2026-04-07 19:48:48] [math_rule_reward_worker.py (162)] [ERROR] [reward-math_rule-1 1 / 4][PID 210653] timeout answer: 70, response: To solve the problem, we need to find the sum of all integer bases \( b > 9 \) for which \( 17_b \) is a divisor of \( 97_b \).
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=210653, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=210653, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m First, let's convert the base-\( b \) numbers to base-10:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=210653, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m - \( 17_b = 1 \cdot b + 7 = b + 7 \)
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=210653, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m - \( 97_b = 9 \cdot b + 7 = 9b + 7 \)
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=210653, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=210653, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m We need \( b + 7 \) to be a divisor of \( 9b + 7 \). This means there exists an integer \( k \) such that:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=210653, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[ 9b + 7 = k(b + 7) \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=210653, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=210653, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m Expanding and rearranging the equation, we get:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=210653, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[ 9b + 7 = kb + 7k \]
