[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m ### Step 1: Find a root modulo 3
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m We evaluate \( f(x) \) at \( x = 0, 1, 2 \) modulo 3:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m f(0) = 0^4 + 10 \cdot 0^2 + 0 + 3 \equiv 3 \equiv 0 \pmod{3}
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m f(1) = 1^4 + 10 \cdot 1^2 + 1 + 3 \equiv 1 + 1 + 1 + 3 \equiv 6 \equiv 0 \pmod{3}
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m f(2) = 2^4 + 10 \cdot 2^2 + 2 + 3 \equiv 16 + 40 + 2 + 3 \equiv 61 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m So, \( x = 0 \) and \( x = 1 \) are roots modulo 3. We will use \( x = 0 \) to lift to a root modulo 9.
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m ### Step 2: Lift the root to modulo 9
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m We need to find \( x_1 \) such that \( x = 3x_1 \) is a root of \( f(x) \) modulo 9. We have:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m f(3x_1) = (3x_1)^4 + 10(3x_1)^2 + 3x_1 + 3 = 81x_1^4 + 90x_1^2 + 3x_1 + 3 \equiv 3x_1 + 3 \pmod{9}
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=736897)[0m We need \( 3x_1 + 3 \equiv 0 \pmod{9} \), which simplifies to \( 3x_1 \equiv -3 \pmod{9} \) or \( x_1 \equiv -1 \pmod{3} \). So, \( x_1 = 2 \) and \( x = 3 \cdot 2 = 6 \) is a root modulo
