[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m First, consider the exponential term \(e^{-\frac{1}{2x^3}}\). For large \(x\), we can use the Taylor series expansion of \(e^y\) around \(y = 0\):
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-0) pid=736610)[0m \[ e^y = 1 + y + \frac{y^2}{2!} + \frac{y^3}{3!} + \cdots \]
